                                                                  2637 1451
 This evaluation has been developed in support of the             2637 1451
 international CIELO project.                                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The evaluation, file assembly and criticality validation         2637 1451
 were performed at BNL and IAEA by                                2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 G.P.A. Nobre, D.A. Brown, M. Herman, R. Arcilla, R. Capote,      2637 1451
 A. Trkov, M. Moxon                                               2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Primary reference: Nucl. Data Sheets 148, 214 (2018)             2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The evaluation was performed up to 150 MeV for 7 subsequent      2637 1451
 neutron emissions, 3 proton emissions and a single alpha-        2637 1451
 particle, deuteron, triton, and 3He for a total of 146           2637 1451
 residual nuclei. Only 5 of these were treated as exclusive,      2637 1451
 i.e., had an explicit reaction assigned to them along with the   2637 1451
 corresponding exclusive spectra for all particles and gammas.    2637 1451
 The exclusive reactions were (n,g), (n,elas), (n,inel), (n,      2637 1451
 2n), (n,p), and (n, 2p). For all the remaining we provide        2637 1451
 cross sections for residua production which represent a sum of   2637 1451
 contributions from all possible reactions populating a given     2637 1451
 residue. Their respective spectra are lumped together            2637 1451
 providing only seven cumulative spectra (for gammas, neutrons,   2637 1451
 protons, alphas, deuterons, tritons, and 3He) for all the        2637 1451
 inclusive channels.                                              2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Development of the evaluation is traced in the                   2637 1451
 CIELO-iron project on the NNDC GForge server:                    2637 1451
 https://ndclx4.bnl.gov/gf/project/cielo_iron                     2637 1451
 All elements of the evaluation, such as experimental datasets,   2637 1451
 EMPIRE inputs and outputs, intermediate ENDF files and plots     2637 1451
 can be followed there in a chronological order.                  2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 EXPERIMENTAL DATBASE                                             2637 1451
 --------------------                                             2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The experimental database used in the evaluation is summarized   2637 1451
 in Table IX of the primary reference (NDS 148,241(2018)). In     2637 1451
 doing this selection we favored: (i) broad energy range covered  2637 1451
 by the experiment, (ii) high energy resolution, and (iii) high   2637 1451
 (but reasonable) accuracy of the experiment. Thus the            2637 1451
 measurements providing possibly wide and reliable picture were   2637 1451
 given precedence over single energy-point experiments,           2637 1451
 especially within the energy range where strong fluctuations     2637 1451
 were observed. On the other hand, many single- or a few-point    2637 1451
 measurements adopted when no extended datasets were              2637 1451
 available. Some of the measured datasets were renormalized to    2637 1451
 account for changes in the reference cross sections since they   2637 1451
 were used in the analysis of the experiments. The                2637 1451
 renormalizations built into the EXFOR retrieval system were      2637 1451
 adopted. In case of the (n,p) and (n,2n) reactions we took       2637 1451
 advantage of the existing high quality evaluations available     2637 1451
 from the IRDFF library (INDC(NDS)-0616, IAEA, Vienna (2012))     2637 1451
 and used these data instead of the actual measurements.          2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 RESOLVED RESONANCE RANGE (adopted from JEFF-3.2)                 2637 1451
 ------------------------------------------------                 2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Reich-Moore resolved resonance parameters covering the energy    2637 1451
 range 1.0e-5 eV to 350 keV were reevaluated by M.C. Moxon [mox05]2637 1451
 under a service agreement with the IAEA (2004).                  2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Thermal cross sections                                           2637 1451
 ----------------------                                           2637 1451
 Thermal cross section values are implicit in the resonance       2637 1451
 parameters evaluated by M.C. Moxon [mox05]. The 2200 m/s cross   2637 1451
 section values for T = 0 are as follows:                         2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
    Total               8.790 b                                   2637 1451
    Elastic scattering  7.476 b                                   2637 1451
    Radiative capture   1.314 b                                   2637 1451
                       (1.30  b  [mug03])                         2637 1451
                       (1.31  b  [mox02])                         2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The expected 1/v dependence of the capture cross section below   2637 1451
 the first positive resonance is accomplished by the addition of  2637 1451
 several negative energy resonances. The radiation width of the   2637 1451
 first bound level was adjusted so to reproduce the evaluated     2637 1451
 thermal capture cross section.                                   2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Resolved Resonance Region (up to 350 KeV)                        2637 1451
 -----------------------------------------                        2637 1451
 Resonance parameters up to 150 keV originate directly from the   2637 1451
 measurements. Between 150 and 350 keV additional resonances for  2637 1451
 l=2,3,4 were added so that they reproduce the observed average   2637 1451
 capture cross sections. The detailed pointwise cross-section     2637 1451
 shapes in this energy range may be incorrect.                    2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The resonance integrals at T=300 K between 0.55 eV and 2 MeV are:2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
    Total               115.5 b                                   2637 1451
    Elastic scattering  113.6 b                                   2637 1451
    Radiative capture   1.246 b                                   2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Reich-Moore parameters were determined from the following        2637 1451
 sources:                                                         2637 1451
                 Capture analysis         Hockenbury+ [hoc69]     2637 1451
    7 - 325 keV  Capture & transmission   Hong+       [hon77]     2637 1451
    0 - 500 keV  Transmission analysis    Garg+       [gar78]     2637 1451
  2.5 - 200 keV  Capture analysis         Allen+      [all80]     2637 1451
   10 - 100 keV      "      "             Kaeppeler+  [kae83]     2637 1451
      ~ 359 eV   Capture analysis         Gayther+    [gay76]     2637 1451
  230 & 359 eV   Capture analysis         Borella+    [bor04]     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The parameters given in the report by Beer et al [bee78] appear  2637 1451
 to be identical to those given in Hong et al's paper. The smooth 2637 1451
 cross section in EXFOR is thought to be a calculation using      2637 1451
 their fitted parameters.                                         2637 1451
   The EXFOR file lists the total cross section measurement by    2637 1451
 Doil'nitsin et al [doi76], but the reference nor any other       2637 1451
 details of the measurement could be obtained, so the data were   2637 1451
 excluded from the analysis.                                      2637 1451
   Further detail on the adjustments and assumptions on the raw   2637 1451
 experimental data can be found in the main reference [mox05].    2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
  REACTION MODELING IN FAST NEUTRON RANGE                         2637 1451
  ---------------------------------------                         2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The fast region calculations were performed using the EMPIRE     2637 1451
 code, which is a comprehensive package that integrates into a    2637 1451
 single system a number of important physical modules and         2637 1451
 evaluation tools. We have adopted coupled channel (CC)           2637 1451
 calculations in the incident channel and for the direct          2637 1451
 inelastic scattering to collective levels. In addition, we       2637 1451
 have allowed for the Distorted Wave Born Approximation (DWBA)    2637 1451
 to populate presumably collective levels which are not           2637 1451
 included in the ground state band used in the CC calculations    2637 1451
 to adjust neutron emission spectra in the high energy range.     2637 1451
 For the preequilibrium (PE) emission employed the classical      2637 1451
 exciton model (PCROSS module) because of its higher              2637 1451
 flexibility in modeling neutron spectra compared with the        2637 1451
 physically more advanced, but also more rigid quantum            2637 1451
 mechanical models. The same module was also used for the PE      2637 1451
 emission of protons and gammas as well as for PE emission of     2637 1451
 clusters, the latter taking advantage of the Iwamoto-Harada      2637 1451
 model.                                                           2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 The Hauser-Feshbach formulation of the statistical model was     2637 1451
 employed to follow sequential emission of particles from the     2637 1451
 compound nucleus (CN). The elastic and inelastic angular         2637 1451
 distributions of neutrons emitted from the CN were calculated    2637 1451
 using the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism. The width fluctuation      2637 1451
 factor was accounted for within HRTW formalism applied up to     2637 1451
 12.0 MeV incident neutrons. Compound nucleus and direct cross    2637 1451
 sections were added incoherently.                                2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Gilbert-Cameron model for level-density has been adopted due     2637 1451
 to it performance on the 56Fe(n,p) reaction that is extremely    2637 1451
 sensitive to level densities. Level density parameters were      2637 1451
 fitted to reproduce the asymptotic behavior of discrete          2637 1451
 levels, as well as to correctly describe the observed cross      2637 1451
 sections.                                                        2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 Complete g-ray cascades for every excited nucleus were           2637 1451
 calculated using M1, E1 and E2 electromagnetic transitions.      2637 1451
 g-strength functions for E1 transitions from the RIPL3 MLO1      2637 1451
 formulation by Pluyko.                                           2637 1451
 Occasionally, we resorted to energy-dependent parameter          2637 1451
 scaling or tuning factors, available in the EMPIRE code, to      2637 1451
 account for model deficiencies. These corrections were           2637 1451
 typically on the level of 1-2% or less and can be found in the   2637 1451
 input files. Whenever possible the reaction model parameters     2637 1451
 used in the 54Fe evaluation were kept consistent with those      2637 1451
 used in the 56Fe evaluation.                                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 FAST NEUTRON RANGE                                               2637 1451
 ------------------                                               2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MF=3 Neutron cross sections                                      2637 1451
 EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data      2637 1451
 were adopted unless stated differently below.                    2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=1 Total                                                       2637 1451
 The cross sections were calculated with the EMPIRE code using    2637 1451
 an optical potential #614 in RIPL-3.                             2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=2 Elastic scattering                                          2637 1451
 Smooth EMPIRE calculations above the resonance region were       2637 1451
 adopted.                                                         2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=4, 51-91 Inelastic scattering                                 2637 1451
 Results of EMPIRE calculations were adopted.                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=5 (n,anything)                                                2637 1451
 Sum of all reactions not given explicitly in another MT          2637 1451
 number as calculated by the EMPIRE code. This MT includes        2637 1451
 majority of the reaction channels - namely all those for which   2637 1451
 we do not provide a complete set of exclusive emission           2637 1451
 spectra. The reason for this unusual representation is a         2637 1451
 difficulty to disentangle contributions of different reactions   2637 1451
 to the gamma spectrum from the final residue. This becomes an    2637 1451
 issue at higher incident energies when multiple reactions        2637 1451
 contribute to the production of a given residue. Note that       2637 1451
 exclusive gamma emission spectrum is required by the ENDF-6      2637 1451
 format for each reaction that is treated as exclusive. In the    2637 1451
 MT=5 representation, the cross sections can be obtained by       2637 1451
 multiplying cumulative cross section given in MT=5 by the        2637 1451
 relative yields for production of the residual nuclei given in   2637 1451
 the subsections of MF/MT=6/5. Particle and gamma emission        2637 1451
 spectra are summed up over the MT=5 reactions into the           2637 1451
 inclusive spectra given in the respective subsections of         2637 1451
 MF/MT=6/5.                                                       2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=102 Capture                                                   2637 1451
 EMPIRE calculations were adopted.                                2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=16 (n,2n)                                                     2637 1451
 Results of EMPIRE calculations were adopted.                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=600-609, 649 (n,p)                                            2637 1451
 Results of EMPIRE calculations were adopted.                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=111, (n,2p)                                                   2637 1451
 Results of EMPIRE calculations were adopted.                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MT=800-809 (n,a_discrete)                                        2637 1451
 Results of EMPIRE calculations were adopted.                     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MF=4 Angular distributions                                       2637 1451
 MT=2 Elastic scattering: EMPIRE calculations were adopted.       2637 1451
 MT=4 Inelastic scattering: EMPIRE calculations were adopted.     2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MF=6 Energy-angle distributions of reaction products             2637 1451
 EMPIRE calculations were adopted                                 2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MF=12 Transition probability arrays for photon production        2637 1451
 Taken from the RIPL library                                      2637 1451
                                                                  2637 1451
 MF=14 Photon angular distributions                               2637 1451
 Isotropic distributions were assumed                             2637 1451
